From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 19:23:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] tests: check NaT with np.isnat() instead of comparing
 reprs

test_convert_to_numpy compares repr(arr[i, 0]) with
repr(np.datetime64('NaT')). Since NumPy 2 the repr of a NaT carries its
unit, so the datetime64[ns] NaT returned by Frame.to_numpy() reprs as
np.datetime64('NaT','ns') while the reference reprs as
np.datetime64('NaT','generic'), and the test fails although the value is
correct. Newer NumPy also deprecates the 'generic' unit, which upstream's
-Werror turns into an error.

Check the two NA cells with np.isnat() instead, which is what the test
means.

Upstream-Status: To upstream [upstream has merged no external code change since the 1.1.0 release]

Signed-off-by: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
---
 tests/types/test-time64.py | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/tests/types/test-time64.py b/tests/types/test-time64.py
index e8038c5..dbd2c8d 100644
--- a/tests/types/test-time64.py
+++ b/tests/types/test-time64.py
@@ -317,9 +317,9 @@ def test_convert_to_numpy(np):
     assert arr.dtype == np.dtype('datetime64[ns]')
     assert arr[0, 0] == np.datetime64('1901-12-13T00:11:59.000000000')
     assert arr[1, 0] == np.datetime64('2001-02-17T00:30:00.000000000')
-    assert repr(arr[2, 0]) == repr(np.datetime64('NaT'))
+    assert np.isnat(arr[2, 0])
     assert arr[3, 0] == np.datetime64('2077-05-17T23:59:01.002345000')
-    assert repr(arr[4, 0]) == repr(np.datetime64('NaT'))
+    assert np.isnat(arr[4, 0])
     assert arr[5, 0] == np.datetime64('1911-11-11T11:11:11.000011000')
 
 
